A threat actor can use the CVE-2021-44228 Apache Log4j vulnerability to execute a malicious payload on a compromised web server by creating a malicious Java-based payload that utilizes the vulnerability to inject arbitrary Java code into the web server. The payload can then be delivered to the compromised web server through a variety of means, including email, phishing, or social engineering.

Once the malicious payload has been delivered to the compromised web server, it can be used to execute arbitrary Java code on the system. The threat actor can then use the executed Java code to extract sensitive data from the system, install malware, or establish a persistent presence on the system.

Mitigation strategies include:

- Implementing security updates for known vulnerabilities
- Conducting regular vulnerability scans to identify and remediate weaknesses
- Implementing web application firewalls (WAFs) to block suspicious traffic
- Implementing secure coding practices to prevent vulnerabilities
- Conducting regular penetration testing to identify and remediate weaknesses

Example of CVE-2021-44228 Apache Log4j vulnerability exploitation:
```java
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.lang.ClassLoader;

public class Log4jExploit {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        File logFile = new File("/var/log/app.log");
        FileWriter writer = new FileWriter(logFile);
        writer.write(" %n");
        writer.close();
        ClassLoader classloader = new Log4jExploitClassLoader();
        Class<?> clazz = classloader.loadClass("java.lang.Shell");
        Object shell = clazz.newInstance();
        System.out.println(shell);
    }
}
```
